@echasnovski another thing that I thought:

A lot of the functionality of mini.completion is duplicating the functionality added in core with `vim.lsp.completion`. The main difference is that mini.completion has proper auto complete, while built-in is only activated on trigger chars.

So perhaps, when 0.11 is released, it would make sense to refactor that plugin to remove all of the custom popup menu item building and rely on `vim.lsp.completion.trigger()` instead? This would simplify the code, fit the plugin's goal of using as much native stuff as possible, and, as  an additional benefit, bring support for snippets and stuff for free.

> Hmmm... Then judging by [LSP specification](https://microsoft.github.io/language-server-protocol/specifications/lsp/3.17/specification/#completionItem), this is not a snippet. A snippet is defined in exactly two ways: either by kind (should be 15; 2 is for "Method") or by `InsertTextFormat` (should be present and equal to 2). I don't think deviating from this specification is a good idea; especially by treating a snippet any item that has tabstop-like text in it.
>
> 
> Or is there an `insertTextFormat` set to 2 in the whole `CompletionList` response (one step higher with respect to nesting)?

I agree. After debugging the `CompletionList`, I found that they do have an `itemDefaults` which includes `insertTextFormat = 2`:
```lua
{
  isIncomplete = false,
  itemDefaults = {
    data = {
      completionKinds = { 2, 3, 6, 9 }
    },
    editRange = {
      ["end"] = {
        character = 16,
        line = 79
      },
      start = {
        character = 15,
        line = 79
      }
    },
    insertTextFormat = 2
  },
  items = { {
      command = {
        arguments = { "13", "0" },
        command = "java.completion.onDidSelect",
        title = ""
      },
      data = {
        pid = "0",
        rid = "13"
      },
      detail = "String.valueOf(double d) : String",
      filterText = "valueOf(${1:0})",
      insertText = "valueOf",
      kind = 2,
      label = "valueOf(double d) : String",
      sortText = "999999179",
      textEditText = "valueOf(${1:0})"
    }
  }
}
```
 Then I lookup into Neovim repo, and in they built-in completion, they handle it like [this](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/blob/c38c88edfd015e8badbd11941030d7d85b5b1dc7/runtime/lua/vim/lsp/completion.lua#L191-L230). Shamelessly, I copied the relevant code over, and now it should close to a reasonable [solution](https://github.com/wenjinnn/mini.nvim/commit/e1089e481ed62f7fbcf2ce3c9e42889ba4cb1760) :)

@wenjinnn, latest `main` should now have support for item defaults. I didn't test it with your case of `jdtls`, but it should work according to specification.

---

Just as a not, there was no update to use `insertText` as `word` field of completion item for snippets (as the is done in the implementation from [this comment](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/886#issuecomment-2707989215)). Mostly because I think this is a bit too cherry-picked to work better in this particular case: other LSP servers might (and probably do) have `insertText` as an actual snippet string which defeats the purpose there.
